    {
      "id": "xxh-3-len-1-to-3-64-b",
      "name": "XXH3_len_1to3_64b",
      "category": "xxh3",
      "signature": "XXH3_len_1to3_64b(Input, Len, Secret, Seed)",
      "params": [
        {
          "name": "Input",
          "type": "xxh_u8*"
        },
        {
          "name": "Len",
          "type": "size_t"
        },
        {
          "name": "Secret",
          "type": "xxh_u8*"
        },
        {
          "name": "Seed",
          "type": "XXH64_hash_t"
        }
      ],
      "returnType": "XXH_FORCE_INLINE XXH64_hash_t",
      "docs": "==========================================\nShort keys\n==========================================\nOne of the shortcomings of XXH32 and XXH64 was that their performance was\nsub-optimal on short lengths. It used an iterative algorithm which strongly\nfavored lengths that were a multiple of 4 or 8.\nInstead of iterating over individual inputs, we use a set of single shot\nfunctions which piece together a range of lengths and operate in constant time.\nAdditionally, the number of multiplies has been significantly reduced. This\nreduces latency, especially when emulating 64-bit multiplies on 32-bit.\nDepending on the platform, this may or may not be faster than XXH32, but it\nis almost guaranteed to be faster than XXH64."
    },
