    {
      "id": "xxh-3-mix-16-b",
      "name": "XXH3_mix16B",
      "category": "xxh3",
      "signature": "XXH3_mix16B(Input, Secret, Seed 64)",
      "params": [
        {
          "name": "Input",
          "type": "xxh_u8* XXH_RESTRICT"
        },
        {
          "name": "Secret",
          "type": "xxh_u8* XXH_RESTRICT"
        },
        {
          "name": "Seed 64",
          "type": "xxh_u64"
        }
      ],
      "returnType": "XXH_FORCE_INLINE xxh_u64",
      "docs": "DISCLAIMER: There are known *seed-dependent* multicollisions here due to\nmultiplication by zero, affecting hashes of lengths 17 to 240.\nHowever, they are very unlikely.\nKeep this in mind when using the unseeded XXH3_64bits() variant: As with all\nunseeded non-cryptographic hashes, it does not attempt to defend itself\nagainst specially crafted inputs, only random inputs.\nCompared to classic UMAC where a 1 in 2^31 chance of 4 consecutive bytes\ncancelling out the secret is taken an arbitrary number of times (addressed\nin XXH3_accumulate_512), this collision is very unlikely with random inputs\nand/or proper seeding:\nThis only has a 1 in 2^63 chance of 8 consecutive bytes cancelling out, in a\nfunction that is only called up to 16 times per hash with up to 240 bytes of\ninput.\nThis is not too bad for a non-cryptographic hash function, especially with\nonly 64 bit outputs.\nThe 128-bit variant (which trades some speed for strength) is NOT affected\nby this, although it is always a good idea to use a proper seed if you care\nabout strength."
    },
