    {
      "id": "xxh-3-accumulate-512",
      "name": "XXH3_accumulate_512",
      "category": "xxh3",
      "signature": "XXH3_accumulate_512(Acc, Input, Secret, Acc Width)",
      "params": [
        {
          "name": "Acc",
          "type": "void* XXH_RESTRICT"
        },
        {
          "name": "Input",
          "type": "void* XXH_RESTRICT"
        },
        {
          "name": "Secret",
          "type": "void* XXH_RESTRICT"
        },
        {
          "name": "Acc Width",
          "type": "XXH3_accWidth_e"
        }
      ],
      "returnType": "XXH_FORCE_INLINE void",
      "docs": "XXH3_accumulate_512 is the tightest loop for long inputs, and it is the most optimized.\nIt is a hardened version of UMAC, based off of FARSH's implementation.\nThis was chosen because it adapts quite well to 32-bit, 64-bit, and SIMD\nimplementations, and it is ridiculously fast.\nWe harden it by mixing the original input to the accumulators as well as the product.\nThis means that in the (relatively likely) case of a multiply by zero, the\noriginal input is preserved.\nOn 128-bit inputs, we swap 64-bit pairs when we add the input to improve\ncross-pollination, as otherwise the upper and lower halves would be\nessentially independent.\nThis doesn't matter on 64-bit hashes since they all get merged together in\nthe end, so we skip the extra step.\nBoth XXH3_64bits and XXH3_128bits use this subroutine."
    },
    {
      "id": "xxh-3-scramble-acc",
      "name": "XXH3_scrambleAcc",
      "category": "xxh3",
      "signature": "XXH3_scrambleAcc(Acc, Secret)",
      "params": [
        {
          "name": "Acc",
          "type": "void* XXH_RESTRICT"
        },
        {
          "name": "Secret",
          "type": "void* XXH_RESTRICT"
        }
      ],
      "returnType": "XXH_FORCE_INLINE void",
      "docs": "XXH3_scrambleAcc: Scrambles the accumulators to improve mixing.\nMultiplication isn't perfect, as explained by Google in HighwayHash:\n// Multiplication mixes/scrambles bytes 0-7 of the 64-bit result to\n// varying degrees. In descending order of goodness, bytes\n// 3 4 2 5 1 6 0 7 have quality 228 224 164 160 100 96 36 32.\n// As expected, the upper and lower bytes are much worse.\nSource: https://github.com/google/highwayhash/blob/0aaf66b/highwayhash/hh_avx2.h#L291\nSince our algorithm uses a pseudorandom secret to add some variance into the\nmix, we don't need to (or want to) mix as often or as much as HighwayHash does.\nThis isn't as tight as XXH3_accumulate, but still written in SIMD to avoid\nextraction.\nBoth XXH3_64bits and XXH3_128bits use this subroutine."
    },
    {
      "id": "xxh-3-accumulate",
      "name": "XXH3_accumulate",
      "category": "xxh3",
      "signature": "XXH3_accumulate(Acc, Input, Secret, Nb Stripes, Acc Width)",
      "params": [
